{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/53771-optimal-book-stack-for-a-new-group","id":53771,"title":"Optimal \"book stack\" for a new group","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-20T00:24:18-08:00","id":53771,"title":"Optimal \"book stack\" for a new group","content":"Hey all, I have a question for forum dwellers who know a lot more about Hunter than myself. I've had the core book since forever (think I bought it when it just came out), and there's Witch Finders and Slashers keeping it company. I've never actually played Hunter, though, and I haven't read the core book from cover to cover. I know what the game's about, obviously, and I've read about the compacts and conspiracies, etc. But, looking at all the books published in the Hunter line so far, I think I'm not really up to date as I though I was. In particular, I somehow managed to not know until this weekend that such a thing as Collection of Horrors even existed, and I only vaguely recall Block by Bloody Block being announced at some point in the past. Why I'm telling you all this is simple - I'm pretty much the dedicated ST (GM, whatever) in my group and Hunter has been voted for the next game we're going to play. So I need to figure out what material should really be required reading before I start running the game...<br /> <br /> One of the players in the group has no experience with RPGs and nother has played RPGs a bit but doesn't have much experience with WoD. So the goal of the chronicle, so to speak, is to make it as independent from other WoD lines as possible. I don't plan on using actual, proper Requiem vampires or Forsaken werewolves. Would, then, Block by Bloody Block be of use in setting up a chronicle? Would you suggest another approach? Any and all suggestions are welcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2050-dawngreeter","name":"Dawngreeter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2050_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"95"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-20T01:30:52-08:00","id":53808,"title":"","content":"my advice is to keep it simple. just go with the core book for starters. maybe bring along &quot;armory&quot; for some ekstra equipment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21-draven","name":"Draven","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-20T08:00:39-08:00","id":53992,"title":"","content":"Consider snagging Antagonists. It's a blue book that should be a great help to any Hunter game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T19:03:26-08:00","id":55117,"title":"","content":"To run the game all you really need is the core book. Armory is good for extra toys though. As an ST I would pick up the Vampire and Werewolf splat so that you have an easy set of go to rules for creating those monsters to use against your players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>in any fight<br /> it is the guy who is willing to die<br /> who is going to win that inch.<br /> <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BlackRoleplayersOrganization\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.facebook.com/BlackRoleplayersOrganization</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1402-kael-winters","name":"Kael Winters","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1402_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-27T13:05:14-08:00","id":59052,"title":"","content":"Personally I found Nightstalkers (the vampire splat) far more useful that Spirit Slayers (The werewolf one) but considering you already have Witchfinders and Slashers it's not completely nessecary. It's a really good book, but between all the ideas in the core book, slashers and Witchfinders you could build a decent campaign.<br /> <br /> Other possible suggestions include Skinchangers (It's the core line shapeshifters book) and Immortals (Also core line deals with things that are immortal), although immortals is thematically quite close to slasher, having three varieties of immortal serial killers.<br /> <br /> Finally as a more specific piece of advice weirdly the place to look for an flexible but simple set of rules for were-creatures is actually the new vampire book, Protean Ranks 2,3 and 4 bascially function as a build your own werecreature rules, maybe add a few more dread powers to flavour. The Designer diaries can be found <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1juI7Er1pUnl79v9zt8NCu4q6jynnCGKJvMZwHJHot7s/pub\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1...MZwHJHot7s/pub</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/711-mcgonigle","name":"mcgonigle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-27T16:01:08-08:00","id":59235,"title":"","content":"I would recommend Block by Bloody Block. It's cheap and useful for city building. The monster books (Skinchangers, Witch Hunter, Spirit Slayers) are all decent and give extra dread powers. Slasher is awesome. One of the best books in new WoD. I would say you don't really need antagonists, because you can already build monsters with dread powers. But I am not really objective, because I found the cults and the hunters from that book too mundane.<br /> <br /> Skinchangers and Immortals are good if you want to throw a curveball at your players. Then again it has a lot of info mostly meant for being relevant if you actually play on of these. I would recommend the Night Horrors series. I like them all except Immortal Sinners.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1873,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1873-jan-paparazzi","user":"Jan Paparazzi","date":". 01-27-2014, 07:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1873-jan-paparazzi","name":"Jan Paparazzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1873_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"257"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}